{"ts":"2026-10-27T10:00:04.159Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_start","contentType":"tool","toolName":"read_files","toolCallId":"xZgzijA4qarLd4U359ToBoL08hoTxZTQ","input":{"files":[{"path":"/app/README.md"}]}}}
{"ts":"2026-10-27T10:00:04.183Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"tool","toolName":"read_files","toolCallId":"xZgzijA4qarLd4U359ToBoL08hoTxZTQ","output":[{"query":"/app/README.md","result":"  1 | # Astrometry toolkit contract and development campaigns\n  2 | \n  3 | Implement the offline astrometry entrypoint at `/app/astrometry/localize.py`.\n  4 | It is invoked without network access as:\n  5 | \n  6 | ```bash\n  7 | python /app/astrometry/localize.py --input-dir /path/to/campaign --output-dir /path/to/output\n  8 | ```\n  9 | \n 10 | The input directory contains `campaign.json`, an `images/` directory, and a\n 11 | catalog referenced by `campaign.json`. Each item in the `images` list has an\n 12 | `image_id`, FITS `path`, `width`, and `height`. Catalog CSV files use the\n 13 | columns `star_id,ra_deg,dec_deg,mag`; `catalog_path` may be relative to the\n 14 | input directory or absolute.\n 15 | \n 16 | Catalogs may be field-local extracts or large shared resources. The example\n 17 | campaign uses a field-local catalog. The global Gaia catalog at\n 18 | `/data/astrometry/gaia_dr3_global.csv` is available for developing and\n 19 | validating bounded-memory indexing and blind-search runtime at scale; its sky\n 20 | coverage is not guaranteed to include the example field, so do not use\n 21 | agreement between those two assets as a correctness check. Each campaign,\n 22 | including catalog indexing and artifact generation, must complete within 15\n 23 | minutes.\n 24 | \n 25 | Optimize toward a reliable, general-purpose astrometry toolkit: reduce catalog\n 26 | reprojection and cross-image alignment errors, improve mosaic overlap\n 27 | agreement, and maximize the fraction of images localized successfully. Use the\n 28 | available data both to develop your solution and to validate that its\n 29 | improvements generalize.\n 30 | \n 31 | Write these artifacts to the output directory:\n 32 | \n 33 | - `wcs/<image_id>.json` for each image, containing `image_id` and a `wcs`\n 34 |   object with two-element `ctype`, `crpix`, and `crval` arrays and a 2x2 `cd`\n 35 |   matrix. The WCS must use a TAN celestial projection.\n 36 | - `registrations.json`, containing a `pairs` array. Each pair identifies\n 37 |   `left` and `right` image IDs and supplies a 3x3 homogeneous `transform`\n 38 |   matrix mapping zero-based pixel coordinates from `left` to `right`. Emit at\n 39 |   most one entry for each unordered overlapping image pair: choose either\n 40 |   direction, but do not also emit the inverse as a second entry.\n 41 | - `mosaic.fits`, containing a finite two-dimensional primary image with a\n 42 |   celestial WCS header.\n 43 | - `run_summary.json`, containing a JSON object.\n 44 | \n 45 | An example input campaign is available at `/app/example_campaign/`. Runtime\n 46 | dependencies listed in `/app/requirements.txt` are already installed.\n 47 | \n 48 | Do not treat one successful example run as sufficient validation. Five varied\n 49 | development regimes and reference samples are available under\n 50 | `/app/development_suite/`. Read this guide before settling on an approach. Fit\n 51 | or tune on a subset, validate on a different regime, and run all five before\n 52 | finishing. The supplied suite includes native and cropped SDSS data, degraded\n 53 | observations, a coarse mixed-survey campaign, and a synthetic star field that\n 54 | requires blind localization against the full-sky Gaia catalog. Design for\n 55 | arbitrary sky fields rather than relying on fixed coordinates from the\n 56 | supplied campaigns.\n 57 | \n 58 | The visible OCI dataset `autonomous-astrometry-toolkit-visible` materializes the\n 59 | already extracted development suite at `/app/development_suite`. No runtime\n 60 | archive extraction or alternate path lookup is required.\n 61 | \n 62 | `/app/development_suite/campaigns/` contains five public campaigns with the same\n 63 | input schema as the task:\n 64 | \n 65 | - `sdss_native`: native-resolution SDSS observations;\n 66 | - `sdss_crop_clean`: offset crops with different image dimensions;\n 67 | - `sdss_crop_degraded`: smaller crops with noise, vignetting, and missing\n 68 |   regions;\n 69 | - `mixed_survey_coarse`: six rotated observations from optical and infrared\n 70 |   surveys at a substantially coarser plate scale; and\n 71 | - `global_catalog_synthetic`: a synthetic public star field rendered from\n 72 |   stars in the agent-visible geometric index, with `catalog_path` set to the\n 73 |   1.35 GB full-sky Gaia catalog. This specifically tests bounded-memory catalog\n 74 |   loading and genuinely blind all-sky indexing; success on a small local\n 75 |   catalog does not validate this path. Use the real-image campaigns to validate\n 76 |   source extraction and this campaign to validate the all-sky lookup path.\n 77 | \n 78 | Each campaign includes `truth/truth.json`. That public truth applies only to\n 79 | these public observations. It contains reference TAN WCS parameters and\n 80 | catalog samples with expected pixel coordinates so you can measure scientific\n 81 | progress. The evaluation campaigns use different sky fields and are not\n 82 | included here.\n 83 | \n 84 | Use the public data for both development and validation. A useful iteration\n 85 | loop is:\n 86 | \n 87 | 1. Fit or tune on one or two campaigns.\n 88 | 2. Keep at least one different regime as a holdout while changing the solver.\n 89 | 3. Run every public campaign before finishing.\n 90 | 4. Inspect the worst image and worst campaign, not only the average.\n 91 | \n 92 | Measure progress with:\n 93 | \n 94 | - the fraction of images with a valid TAN WCS;\n 95 | - median and 90th-percentile reprojection error on the public truth samples;\n 96 | - forward and inverse residuals for each overlapping image registration. Emit\n 97 |   only one direction per unordered pair in `registrations.json`; compute the\n 98 |   inverse in your own validation rather than submitting a second reverse pair;\n 99 | - signal and source agreement where reprojected images overlap in the mosaic;\n100 | - complete, schema-valid artifacts and a zero process exit for every campaign.\n101 | \n102 | After each development run, use the structural checker:\n103 | \n104 | ```bash\n105 | python /app/astrometry/validate_outputs.py \\\n106 |   --input-dir /app/development_suite/campaigns/<campaign> \\\n107 |   --output-dir <output-directory>\n108 | ```\n109 | \n110 | It checks the documented artifact schema, rejects duplicate unordered\n111 | registration pairs, and catches missing, non-finite, or effectively constant\n112 | mosaics. It is a structural check: it does not use public truth or estimate\n113 | scientific quality.\n114 | \n115 | Use these diagnostics during development, but avoid hard-coding public image\n116 | IDs, dimensions, pointings, catalog order, or WCS values. Confirm that changing\n117 | image order, image IDs, catalog row order, and finite intensity scale does not\n118 | materially change results.","success":true}],"durationMs":23}}
